{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794543855, "title": "dev/photo-rename \u00b7 ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Write a Python script that renames my photos by the date they were taken\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[8.46, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/rename_photos.py)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[12.763, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the script to `<workspace>/rename_photos.py`. I haven't run it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Date source:** it uses the EXIF \"date taken\" tag. If a photo has none, it uses the file's modified time.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Reading EXIF needs Pillow (`pip install Pillow`). Without Pillow, every photo falls back to the modified\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  time, and the script prints a note saying so.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **File types:** it handles jpg, jpeg, png, tif, tiff, heic and webp. Pillow may not be able to read HEIC\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  files without an extra plugin, so those may also fall back to the modified time.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Naming:** files become names like `2024-07-14_15-32-08.jpg`. If two photos get the same name, the second\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  becomes `..._1.jpg`, and so on. Nothing is overwritten.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Preview first:** by default it only prints what it would rename. Add `--apply` to rename for real.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```bash\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  python3 rename_photos.py ~/Pictures/trip            # preview\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  python3 rename_photos.py ~/Pictures/trip --apply    # do it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  python3 rename_photos.py ~/Pictures/trip -r --format \"%Y%m%d_%H%M%S\"   # subfolders, custom name format\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Renaming can't be undone by the script, so run the preview first. Do that, or keep a backup, before using\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `--apply`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[12.865, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Before you finish:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +286 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[16.431, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && mkdir -p .ambition/first && cp rename_photos.py .ambition/first/ && ls .ambi…\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[19.186, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I copied the script to `.ambition/first/rename_photos.py`. There's no `.gitignore` here, so I didn't change\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Do you want to stop here or push it further?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stop here:** keep the script as it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Push it further:** I'd go after three things:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    1. Test it on sample photos with known EXIF dates, which I haven't done yet. That includes HEIC files,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  which Pillow can't read without a plugin.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    2. Add an undo log, so a bad `--apply` can be reversed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    3. Handle photos with no EXIF date better, such as using the date in the filename and handling time zones.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    This would take a few rounds of questions and testing. That's more than the single script written so far,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  but still small.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[23.55, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[27.267, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 2/8 · Altitude\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Question 1 of up to 4: what is this script ultimately for, and what should happen once your photos are\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  renamed?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. One-off tidy-up.** Rename an existing folder once, then you're done.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. Ongoing import.** Run it each time you copy photos off a phone or camera, so the library stays\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  organised.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. Archive or backup.** Prepare photos for long-term storage or a service like iCloud, Google Photos or\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Lightroom, where the names or dates must stay correct.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D. Something else.** Tell me.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[32.125, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ B, an ongoing import. I copy photos off my phone and camera into a folder every so often, so I'd run it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  again and again on the same folder. It should skip files that are already renamed and not clobber anything\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  if two photos share the same timestamp.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[38.301, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Noted. Two requirements come out of that: re-running must be safe (already-renamed files are left alone),\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and same-timestamp photos must never overwrite each other. The current script mostly does both, but I\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  haven't tested either, and I'll check them properly.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Question 2 of up to 4: what would make this unmistakably excellent for your routine? Pick the hardest target\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  you'd actually want.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. Safe and boring.** Running it twice in a row changes nothing the second time. Burst shots at the same\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  second get a stable order (`_1`, `_2`) that doesn't reshuffle between runs. Nothing is ever lost.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. A, plus duplicates.** If you copy the same photo off the phone twice, it's detected by content and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  skipped or flagged instead of becoming `_1`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. B, plus an undo log.** Every run records what it renamed, so you can reverse a run with one command.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D. C, plus a fix for photos with the wrong or missing date.** This covers photos with no EXIF date, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  photos from different cameras whose clocks or time zones disagree.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[43.564, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ D. I want the full thing, because I'll be running this on years of photos from my phone and an older camera,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  and some have no date or the wrong one. Safe re-runs and duplicate detection matter most. The undo log is a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  must too, since I'd be nervous renaming thousands of files without a way back. For missing dates, fall back\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  to the file's modified time but flag those files so I can check them.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[46.842, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Noted. Priorities are safe re-runs and duplicate detection first, then the undo log. Missing dates fall back\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  to modified time, and those files get flagged for you to check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Question 3 of up to 4: what would prove it works, other than me saying so?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. A test suite I build.** It would use synthetic photos with known EXIF dates, covering:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - missing dates\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - same-second bursts\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - exact duplicates\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - re-runs\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - undo\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. A, plus a dry run on a copy of your real photos.** You'd point me at a folder copy, and I'd check\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  that a run followed by an undo restores every file byte for byte.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. B, plus your own spot check.** The script writes a report of the flagged files, and you review a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sample before trusting it on the originals.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[51.808, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ B. I'd like the synthetic test suite, and I'd also like you to run it on a copy of a real photo folder and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  confirm that a run followed by an undo restores every file exactly. I'll do my own spot check of the flagged\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  files, so the script should write a readable report listing them, but I don't need you to build anything\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  beyond that.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[56.979, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Noted: a synthetic test suite, a run-then-undo check on a copy of a real folder, and a readable report of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the flagged files. I won't build anything beyond that.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Question 4 of 4: what access am I missing? I need a folder of real photos to test on. I'll only ever work on\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  a copy and never touch the originals.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. You give me a path.** Tell me which folder to copy, ideally a few hundred photos with a mix of phone\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and camera shots.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. You copy a sample in yourself.** Drop a sample into a folder such as `<workspace>/sample_photos` and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tell me when it's there.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. Skip the real-folder check for now.** I build and run only the synthetic suite, and you run the real-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  folder check yourself later.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll also install Pillow (`pip install Pillow`) for EXIF reading unless you'd rather I didn't, and add\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `pillow-heif` only if your sample has HEIC files.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[62.264, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Go with B. I'll copy a sample into `<workspace>/sample_photos`, a mix of phone and camera shots, including\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  some HEIC. Installing Pillow is fine, and add `pillow-heif` since the sample has HEIC files. Work only on a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  copy and leave my originals alone. Go ahead and finish the work without stopping.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[125.7, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Here is the restatement for this step. Since you said to finish without stopping, I'm treating it as\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  confirmed and going straight to the build.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You asked for:   a Python script that renames my photos by the date they were taken\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Raises all of:   - Renaming by date taken: EXIF first, and flagged and plausibility-checked when the date is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  missing or wrong\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Safe for repeated runs on the same folder: nothing already renamed is touched, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  nothing is ever overwritten\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Same-timestamp photos get stable _1, _2 suffixes. Exact duplicates are found by content\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hash.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - A one-command undo log. A readable report of flagged files.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Genre bar:       a command-line tool that a nervous person trusts with thousands of irreplaceable files:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  dry-run by default,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   plain-language output, no surprises, and a report a human can skim\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Aiming at:       a photo-import routine you can run on every import without thinking, because re-runs and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  undo are safe\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Excellent means: - Run, re-run and undo leave the folder byte-for-byte identical (names, contents and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  modified times)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Zero overwrites and zero deletions under any collision\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Every non-EXIF date appears in the report\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Proven by:       - A synthetic unittest suite\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - A run, then undo, on a copy of your sample_photos folder, comparing hashes before and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  after\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Comparison with the first version, which had no undo, no duplicate detection, and no\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  flagging\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Needs access to: Pillow and pillow-heif (installing), plus your sample_photos folder (you're copying it in)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Mode:            build · you answered · no review gate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[126.515, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd /tmp && python3 -m pip install --quiet Pillow pillow-heif 2>&1 | tail -3; python3 -c \"import …\u001b[0m\r\n"]
