[560.225, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ All 30 tests pass, and every mutant is caught, including the one that exposed the old undo gap. I'll add one\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  more test that simulates a lying directory listing, then clean up and save the step.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[562.872, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && python3 - <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[572.763, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Step 3 is done: the script now holds up against a crash at any point, a lying directory listing, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  other real-world failures I tried. All 31 tests pass. The sample-folder check still hasn't happened, because\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `sample_photos` still doesn't exist.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I did**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Real photos:** I ran the date reader over 400 real photos on this machine. exiftool isn't installed, so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I used a direct look at the EXIF tags instead, and the `sips` and Spotlight tools turned out not to be\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  usable as cross-checks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Companion files:** a Live Photo's `.MOV`, `.AAE` and `.xmp` sidecars, and the RAW file of a RAW+JPG\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pair, now get renamed with their photo and share its `_1`, `_2` suffix. Before, the photo was renamed and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the companion was left behind.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Burst order:** same-second photos are now ordered by their sub-second EXIF field, so `_1`, `_2` follow\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the real shot order.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Adversarial tests:** a simulated hard crash at every point of a run, followed by an undo, plus a random-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sequence test over 150 seeds, a hard-link test and a lying-listing test.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Mutation check:** I broke the script on purpose in 8 ways that would lose or misplace photos, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  suite caught every one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I found**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Unreliable EXIF dates:** 45 of the 400 files carry only the EXIF \"last changed\" date. In every file that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  also had the original date, the two differed by months. The script now uses that date only as a last resort\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and flags the file.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Broken EXIF values:** one real file had a time of `14:05:92`. The report now says \"malformed\" instead of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \"no EXIF date\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Silent undo:** undo right after a no-op run would silently reverse an older run. It now prints which run\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it is reversing and when.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Half-finished renames:** killing the script mid-run on this VirtioFS volume left some files with two\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  names. Undo now finishes those repairs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Unreliable listings:** after that kill, the volume's directory listing omitted 105 files that still\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  existed. Nothing was lost. The script now confirms any name it thinks is free with a direct lookup, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  checks every rename afterwards.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Speed:** a preview of 3,000 files took 4 seconds and an apply took 7. A re-run with nothing to do took 0.2\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  seconds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What changed in my plan:** I had planned to cross-check dates against an independent reader, but none of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the available ones worked. The oracle is now the invariant tests plus the mutation check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Snapshots are in `.ambition/steps/3/`. `.ambition/first/` still holds the original script.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[572.857, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 4 of pushing the work further: Challenge. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 4/8 · Challenge`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[605.562, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 4/8 · Challenge\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Assumptions I'm making, riskiest first**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | # | Assumption | Set by | Depends on |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | A | You'll run the script with a Python that has Pillow and pillow-heif. | Me. I told you to run `python3\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rename_photos.py`. | Your Python setup |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | B | `-r` is safe on any folder. | Me | What lives inside your photo folders |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | C | Pillow can open every photo, including panoramas. | Me | Your camera files |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | D | A camera date from 1995 onward is a real date. | Me. I picked 1995 arbitrarily. | Your older camera's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  clock behaviour |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | E | The \"already named\" check never matches a camera's own filenames. | Me | Phone and camera naming\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  schemes |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | F | Your photos carry EXIF dates, and the sample is representative. | You | The sample folder |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll test them against the real thing, starting with A.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
