[754.824, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Step 4 is done: I tested six assumptions and four of them failed on the real thing. All four are fixed, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  all 38 tests pass. The sample-folder check still hasn't happened, because `sample_photos` still doesn't\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  exist.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Assumptions tested, riskiest first**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | # | Assumption | Result |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | A | You'd run the script with a Python that has Pillow and pillow-heif. | **Failed.** With the `python3`\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  command I gave you, every photo was renamed by the time it was copied, even a JPEG with a good EXIF date,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and the only warning was one line. The same happened to HEIC files without pillow-heif. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | B | `-r` is safe on any folder. | **Failed.** It renamed a file inside a Photos library package, which\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  would corrupt the library. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | C | Pillow can open every photo. | **Failed.** A panorama over about 179 megapixels lost its EXIF date. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | D | A camera date from 1995 onward is a real date. | **Weak.** I picked 1995 arbitrarily and found no data\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  to test it, so it's unproven. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | E | The \"already renamed\" check never matches camera filenames. | **Holds.** I tried 15 phone and camera\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  naming schemes, and none matched. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | F | The sample is representative. | **Untestable** until you copy it in. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The blind spot, which failed too:** your top priority was duplicate detection, but it only compared photos\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with the same date name. A photo with no EXIF, like a WhatsApp image, gets a name from its copy time. So\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  importing the same file twice, on two different days, gave two copies with different names and no duplicate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  flagged. I reproduced that on the real script.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I fixed**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A:** without Pillow, or without pillow-heif when HEIC files are present, the script now refuses and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  changes nothing. It prints the install steps. `--allow-mtime` overrides this on purpose.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B:** the script never enters `.photoslibrary` packages (or similar library packages), and refuses if you\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  point it at one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C:** the pixel-size guard is off, since the script never decodes pixels.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Blind spot:** duplicates are now found by content across different names, within a run and across runs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  When a run finds only duplicates, it now writes a report listing them.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D:** dates just after midnight on Jan 1, which is the usual signature of a reset camera clock, are kept\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  but flagged for you to check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I added 7 tests, one per failure plus the clock-reset flag. I also broke the script on purpose in 10 ways,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and the tests caught 9. The tenth was redundant code, not a gap.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Snapshots are in `.ambition/steps/4/`, and `.ambition/first/` is untouched.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[754.925, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 5 of pushing the work further: Range. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 5/8 · Range`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[805.977, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 5/8 · Range\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What this is really trying to do, as verbs.** The script has to:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - read when a photo was really taken\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - name each file by that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - keep a library consistent across repeated imports\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - never lose or overwrite anything\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - let a nervous person see what will happen, check what did, and reverse it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The genre's own verbs are the command-line tool's: do exactly what it says, explain itself, and default to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  safe.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fields that do these verbs differently**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Field | What they actually do | Direction taken |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Command-line tool maintainer** (the genre's best practitioner: rsync, git, `mv -i`) | A dry-run default\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with an explicit apply step *is* the confirmation. They refuse nonsensical targets and fail with clear\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  messages instead of tracebacks. | Refuse dangerous targets (`/`, your home folder with `-r`), and turn\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  permission errors into plain messages. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Digital archivist** | Fixity: record a fingerprint of every file, and verify before trusting a restore.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | The undo log records each file's size and modified time. Undo checks them and refuses to restore a file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  that isn't the one it renamed. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Database engineer** (journaling, locking) | Write-ahead log, which I already have. Plus exclusive locks\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and atomic compare-and-set instead of check-then-act. | A lock so two runs can't collide, and an atomic no-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  clobber rename so a name taken at the last instant can't be overwritten. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Forensic examiner** (the field I wouldn't normally consider) | Record where every conclusion came from,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  so a reviewer can audit it. | Every line in the report says which EXIF tag, or which fallback, produced that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  file's date. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | **Red team** | Break it and find who gets hurt. | Below. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge I'm taking: trust.** Of all the people this serves, the one who benefits most is the nervous one\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with thousands of irreplaceable files. They don't need more features. They need every claim the tool makes\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  to be checkable and every dangerous input refused.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll run the red-team attacks for real first.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[812.783, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && rm -rf /tmp/rt && mkdir -p /tmp/rt && cat > /tmp/rt/attacks.py <<'EOF')\u001b[0m\r\n"]
