[2597.361, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && ls sample_photos 2>&1 | head -2; .venv/bin/python .ambition/judge/acceptance…\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2652.473, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Step 6 is done: the final script passes all 20 acceptance checks and all 50 unit tests. The first version\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  passes 7 of 20 on the same checks. The sample-folder check still hasn't happened, because `sample_photos`\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  still doesn't exist.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The outside check.** `.ambition/judge/acceptance.py` runs a script the way you would, as a separate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  process. It compares the result against reference answers I don't control:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **EXIF dates:** the dates I wrote into the files.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Real photos:** on a copy of 400 real photos from this machine, a name's date must literally appear in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the file's own EXIF bytes, or the file must be flagged. Apply followed by undo must restore every file byte\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  for byte.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Real crashes:** a real `kill -9` partway through a 2,500-file run, then undo.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What it showed**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Original ask and restatement:** all kept and passing. That covers renaming by date taken, no overwrites,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  re-runs that change nothing, same-second suffixes, duplicates, an exact undo, flagged dates, and Live Photo\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  videos staying paired.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **First version:** it passes 7 of 20. It has no undo, no duplicate detection and no report, and it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  overwrote nothing but renamed an already-named file. It also names everything by file time when Pillow is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  missing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Speed:** the check exposed a slowdown I had introduced. The duplicate check compared files pairwise, so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3,000 photos took 14s. I replaced it with a hash index, and the same job now takes 3.3s, with a re-run at\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  0.1 to 0.2s.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Where the check itself was wrong.** My first kill test passed without testing anything, because the kill\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  landed before any file was renamed. After I fixed that, recovery looked like it failed on the work volume.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The cause was the volume settling a few last files' modified times about 33 microseconds late, so my check\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  was reading them too early. I kept the settle-before-snapshot fix, and every real mid-run kill (12 across\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  two full runs, on both volumes) was restored exactly.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Cold read.** I read the help text, preview, apply, re-run, undo and error messages whole, and fixed what I\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  found:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The summary said \"date not from EXIF\" over a file whose date did come from EXIF.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Every re-run that found duplicates wrote another identical report. Now one duplicates-only report is kept.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - `-r` and `FOLDER` had no help text.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - \"Never deletes a file\" was slightly loose; it now says \"never deletes a photo\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The \"Not a directory\" message printed a rewritten path instead of the one you typed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Still unproven:** your own photos. The check used synthetic files and 400 real system photos, not an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  iPhone and camera library.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Snapshots are in `.ambition/steps/6/`, and `.ambition/first/` is untouched.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2652.568, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 7 of pushing the work further: Stretch. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[2663.585, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The target.** Your brief was to rename thousands of photos safely, and the fallback I hold myself to is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the current state: 3,000 photos apply in 3.3s, and every safety check passes. The stretch is five times\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  faster. **3,000 photos should apply in under 0.7s, and 20,000 in under 5s**, with every one of the 50 unit\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tests and 20 acceptance checks still passing. Someone has done this: exiftool and rsync handle tens of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  thousands of files per second by batching their I/O and doing the metadata reads in parallel.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Where it would likely break: the safety guarantee that each log entry reaches disk before its rename. That\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  guarantee is why a crash is recoverable, so I won't trade it for speed. I'll profile first.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
