[3264.909, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Step 7 is done: both stretch targets are met, and all 54 unit tests and all 20 acceptance checks still pass.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The sample-folder check still hasn't happened, because `sample_photos` still doesn't exist.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The target.** Your brief was to rename thousands of photos safely. The stretch was about five times faster\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  than where the script stood, and the fallback was to keep every safety check passing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Test | Before this step | Stretch target | Now |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 3,000 photos, apply | 3.3s | under 0.7s | **0.52s** |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 20,000 photos, apply | about 8 minutes (estimated from the quadratic growth) | under 5s | **3.8s** |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Re-run with nothing to do | 0.2s | n/a | 0.04s at 3,000, 0.3s at 20,000 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Undo | n/a | n/a | 0.24s at 3,000, 2.1s at 20,000 |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  For comparison, the first version took 2.7s for 20,000 photos. It has no undo, no duplicate check, no log\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and no lock, so the final is slower there, but it's the same order of magnitude and does far more.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I found and did**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A real scaling bug.** The companion-file lookup scanned the whole folder for every photo, so the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  planning step was quadratic. My earlier 3,000-file timings hid it. At 20,000 files it would have taken\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  minutes. It now uses a one-time index.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Second duplicate-check bug.** The duplicate index re-walked every same-size file on each call. It now\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hashes each file once.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Log writes.** The undo log is now written and flushed in batches of 100 renames, with every entry on\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  disk before its rename. Crash safety is unchanged, and the cost is one disk flush per batch.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Smaller savings.** Cheaper path handling, and the \"is this inside the folder\" check once per directory.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What didn't hold up:** reading EXIF on several threads. It was slower at every thread count (one thread:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  0.53s and 4.29s; eight threads: 0.64s and 4.86s) because the interpreter lock dominates. I removed it, so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the deliverable is not worse for the attempt.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Safety re-proven.** Batching changed the crash path, so I broke it on purpose. Five of my mutants survived\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  at first, because my in-process crash simulation can't see an unflushed log. I added a test that hard-kills\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  a child process, plus tests for the two new code paths, and all the mutants are caught now. Real `kill -9`\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tests also recovered exactly, including two 20,000-file runs killed with 8,622 and 8,120 files already\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  renamed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Where it would break.** Past roughly 100,000 files in one folder, the in-memory plan and the per-folder\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  name index will start to matter. I didn't test that scale.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Snapshots are in `.ambition/steps/7/`, and `.ambition/first/` is untouched.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
