[3396.927, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ## Plan of record\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Original ask.** A Python script that renames photos by the date they were taken. `rename_photos.py` does\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  this by reading the EXIF date and naming each file `YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS.ext`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Goal beyond the goal.** You import photos from a phone and an older camera into the same folder,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  repeatedly, over years of photos. The script has to be safe to run on every import without thinking. That\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  means re-runs change nothing, nothing is ever lost or overwritten, duplicates are caught, and any run can be\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  undone.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Target and fallback.**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stretch target:** 3,000 photos in under 0.7s and 20,000 in under 5s.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Fallback:** any speed that keeps every safety check passing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Approach and instruments**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Dates:** EXIF taken, then digitized, then the \"file changed\" tag, then file time. Anything not from the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  first two, or that looks like a camera clock reset, is flagged in a readable report.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Safety:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Each rename is logged to disk before it happens.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Renames are atomic and never replace a file.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - One lock per folder.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Undo verifies that a file is the one it renamed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Duplicates and pairs:** found by content hash. Live Photo videos, sidecars and RAW files move with their\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  photo.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Proof tools:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - 54 unit tests.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - A 20-check acceptance script with real `kill -9` runs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - A deliberate-breakage check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Assumptions tested.** Six, and four failed: the Python setup, `-r` into photo libraries, huge panoramas,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and duplicate detection for photos without EXIF. Two held: camera filenames never look \"already renamed\",\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and Unicode names round-trip. All four failures are fixed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Borrowed methods.** The command-line tool maintainer's dry-run default and clean refusals. The archivist's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  identity check before restoring. The database engineer's write-ahead log and lock. The forensic examiner's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  record of where each date came from.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Outside check.** The acceptance script, compared against the first version.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Locked in.** The script, tests, acceptance check, requirements file, a CLAUDE.md note and a `/rename-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  photos` command.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Does this stand as the plan of record? The work it describes is built and tested.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ---\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Report\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **You asked for → it became**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - *Rename photos by date taken* became a script that reads the real EXIF date and names files by it. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  date source is recorded per file, and anything doubtful is flagged.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - *Re-run safely on the same folder* became a no-op on re-runs, atomic no-clobber renames, one lock per\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  folder, and refusal to start without Pillow or HEIC support.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - *Don't clobber same-timestamp photos* became stable `_1`, `_2` suffixes ordered by sub-second shot order,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with companion files kept together.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - *Duplicate detection* became content-hash detection across different names, with and without EXIF.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Duplicates are left in place and reported.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - *Undo log* became an exact undo of names, bytes and modified times. It verifies each file, refuses\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tampered logs, and recovers from a hard crash.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - *Fall back to modified time but flag it* became a flag for that case, for dates that look wrong, and for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  dates just after a Jan 1 midnight (a likely clock reset), all in a readable report.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Against the first version**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Better** on every part of the ask and the goal. The acceptance check scored the first version **7/20**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and the final **20/20**.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Gave up:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Speed at 20,000 files: 3.8s against 2.7s for the first version, which has no log, no undo and no\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  duplicate check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - It now refuses instead of guessing when Pillow or pillow-heif is missing; `--allow-mtime` overrides\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  this.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - It refuses `-r` on your home folder, the filesystem root and photo-library packages.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Outside check.** All of these ran and passed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Unit tests:** 54 pass.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Acceptance script:** 20 of 20, including 12 real mid-run `kill -9` recoveries and a round trip on a copy\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  of about 400 real macOS photos.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Deliberate breakage:** 12 or more ways of breaking the script were caught by the tests.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Large crash recovery:** two kills at 20,000 files restored exactly.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Not yet run on your photos.**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Stretch.** 3,000 photos in **0.52s** and 20,000 in **3.8s**, so both targets were met. It broke in two\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  places:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Threaded EXIF reading** was slower at every thread count, so I removed it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Past roughly 100,000 files in one folder** is untested.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Locked in** (in `<workspace>`)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Files: `rename_photos.py`, `test_rename_photos.py`, `acceptance_check.py`, `requirements.txt`,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/commands/rename-photos.md`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Setup once: `python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Rerun the proof: `.venv/bin/python -m unittest test_rename_photos` (3s) and `.venv/bin/python\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  acceptance_check.py` (about 3 minutes).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Snapshots are in `.ambition/steps/1` to `8`; `first/` holds the original. The snapshot in `steps/8/` has\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  everything except the command file, because copying into a `.claude` folder needed permission I didn't have.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The command file itself exists at `.claude/commands/rename-photos.md`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Assumptions made.** You told me to finish without stopping, so I accepted the restatement myself:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I treated time zones and camera clock offsets as out of scope.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I chose 1995 as the earliest believable year.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I chose \"Jan 1, first 10 minutes after midnight\" as the clock-reset signature.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I used a project virtual environment.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I built for macOS.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Decisions left**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | # | Decision | Options | For / against | My pick | Owner, date |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 1 | Prove it on your photos | A: you copy 200 to 500 mixed phone and camera photos (some HEIC) into\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `sample_photos` and I run apply and undo and compare hashes. B: you run the preview on a copy and review the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  flagged list yourself. | A is exact but needs you to copy files in. B is quick but relies on your eye. | A\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  first, then B | You copy by **2026-11-20**; I run it within a day of that |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 2 | Wrong camera clock on the older camera | A: ignore (today). B: add a `--shift` offset for a folder. C:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  use the time-zone offset EXIF field when present. | B fixes a consistent offset but is another option to get\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  wrong. C is automatic but older cameras rarely write it. | A until the spot check shows a consistent offset,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  then B | You, after your spot check, by **2026-11-27** |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 3 | What to do with duplicates | A: leave in place (today). B: move them to a `_duplicates` folder, logged\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and undoable. C: delete them. | A is safest but needs manual cleanup. B is tidy and reversible. C is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  irreversible. | B, after seeing how many the first real import finds | You decide by **2026-11-27**; I build\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 4 | Photos managed by Lightroom, Capture One or Photos | A: rename outside the app (today, with a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  warning). B: add an import mode that copies photos into a new folder. | A breaks catalog links. B costs disk\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  space and is a new feature. | A unless your photos live in a catalog | You, by **2026-11-20** |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Next checks**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - After the first real import, review the flagged list in `_rename_logs/*.report.txt`. Owner: you, by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **2026-11-20**.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Before shipping any change, re-run both proof commands. Owner: whoever makes the change.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
